00:00:41.938 --> 00:00:47.051
For an activity that mostly 
involves sitting and staring,

00:00:47.051 --> 00:00:51.537
microscopy is a surprisingly high stakes task.

00:00:51.537 --> 00:00:56.113
On the other side of the lens 
are drops full of potential,

00:00:56.113 --> 00:01:00.330
a multitude of worlds to unravel and examine.

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:08.829
But they’re also fragile worlds, easy to
fracture and lose with just a tiny slip of the hand.

00:01:08.829 --> 00:01:16.833
The stakes only get higher when you’re dealing
with an organism like this Dactylochlamys pisciformis,

00:01:16.833 --> 00:01:21.539
a creature so rare that it’s only been 
reported a few times since it was

00:01:21.539 --> 00:01:24.000
first discovered in 1901.

00:01:24.654 --> 00:01:30.679
This was one of those rare sightings, a
clip we showed in a previous episode because

00:01:30.679 --> 00:01:35.162
we were so excited to find something so unlikely.

00:01:35.162 --> 00:01:41.139
Watching it swim around on its own, oblivious
to our presence, it seems like this is what

00:01:41.139 --> 00:01:45.440
spotting a solitary tiger in the wild would
feel like.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:50.784
Distant, unique, and all the more precious
for its rarity.

00:01:50.784 --> 00:01:57.799
But one day, our singular microbial friend
vanished without a trace, leaving a trail

00:01:57.799 --> 00:02:01.332
of mysteries and inspiration in its wake.

00:02:01.332 --> 00:02:09.950
Finding this one Dactylochlamys pushed James,
our master of microscopes, to try and find more.

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:16.360
So he went back to the spot where he’d found
it, sampling the same waters over and over

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:24.157
again so that he had drops and drops to sift
and sort through for hundreds of hours on end.

00:02:24.157 --> 00:02:28.634
But for months, nothing turned up,

00:02:28.634 --> 00:02:32.808
until finally, one did.

00:02:32.808 --> 00:02:36.610
The sight had James’ hands shaking with
excitement.

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:41.183
It’s one thing to put so much work into
finding a rare organism.

00:02:41.183 --> 00:02:45.938
But the reality of success, that’s something
else altogether.

00:02:45.938 --> 00:02:51.920
And all of those feelings, the thrill of
discovery, the satisfaction of work paying

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:59.913
off, they were, in this case, also interlaced
with the fragility of what James had found.

00:02:59.913 --> 00:03:06.650
On the one hand, he wanted to be able to watch
it live its life and record those moments,

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:12.930
capture them so that its morphology and behavior
could be etched into a vivid record for scientists

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:16.250
to study and draw conclusions from.

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:18.380
But video is risky.

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:24.540
To be able to see so deeply into the organism,
James has to watch it with the objective close

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:29.583
to the sample, which leaves room for only
a thin layer of water

00:03:29.583 --> 00:03:32.082
for the Dactylochlamys to survive in.

00:03:32.082 --> 00:03:38.240
And sometimes, they don’t survive, which
means we would lose both the organism and

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:41.130
the possibility of learning more about it.

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:46.970
We’d already lost one Dactylochlamys, we
didn’t want to lose another.

00:03:46.970 --> 00:03:55.160
So James was very, very careful, transferring
the ciliate from the larger sample to a slide

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:58.650
and then gently covering it with the coverslip.

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:06.040
He added a drop of immersion oil to the coverslip
and dipped his best microscope objective into the oil.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:12.907
And then he pressed record, gathering around
25 minutes in the life of a rare microbe.

00:04:17.905 --> 00:04:25.503
In 1901, Robert Lauterborn, the German biologist
who discovered Dactylochlamys pisciformis, gave

00:04:25.503 --> 00:04:31.991
what is perhaps the best description of the
organism, saying “…it is one of the most

00:04:31.991 --> 00:04:35.409
bizarre and striking microbes I know….”

00:04:36.234 --> 00:04:37.919
And he’s right.

00:04:37.919 --> 00:04:44.389
This might be a strange reference to make
for an organism we were excited to find, but

00:04:44.389 --> 00:04:50.729
there’s something about this Dactylochlamys
that resembles a lopsided Eye of Sauron.

00:04:50.729 --> 00:04:56.990
It’s that combination of the elongated shape,
the large round contractile vacuole on the

00:04:56.990 --> 00:05:01.868
posterior side of its body, and the clutter
of structures that project

00:05:01.868 --> 00:05:04.357
out like the spikes of a fire.

00:05:04.357 --> 00:05:11.864
Maybe a better comparison is this Actinobolina,
another ciliate that we’ve talked about on this channel.

00:05:11.864 --> 00:05:17.409
For the actinobolina, those tentacle-like
spikes have a clear purpose.

00:05:17.409 --> 00:05:20.844
They help the organism trap food and feed.

00:05:20.844 --> 00:05:26.364
So perhaps the Dactylochlamys uses its own
spikes for a similar purpose.

00:05:26.364 --> 00:05:30.872
But until we see them in action, we won’t
know for sure.

00:05:30.872 --> 00:05:34.540
But James also noticed something 
when he was comparing

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:38.080
this second Dactylochlamys to the
first one he found.

00:05:38.605 --> 00:05:41.254
They didn’t quite look the same.

00:05:41.254 --> 00:05:47.659
There were subtle differences in their macronucleus
and cilia and other parts that made him think

00:05:47.659 --> 00:05:54.400
that maybe they’re different organisms, that
maybe the organism he found last year was

00:05:54.400 --> 00:06:00.908
actually not a Dactylochlamys pisciformis,
but actually a related species called

00:06:00.908 --> 00:06:09.791
Dactylochlamys hystriz that was discovered in 1928 
and then never found again, until James.

00:06:11.717 --> 00:06:15.889
Because both of these species 
have been so difficult to find,

00:06:15.889 --> 00:06:19.364
their descriptions are sparse and incomplete.

00:06:19.364 --> 00:06:25.449
So for now, we believe that the first one
that James found was Dactylochlamys hystriz,

00:06:25.449 --> 00:06:29.180
and the second one was a Dactylochlamys pisciformis.

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:34.385
And James is working on uncovering as much
as he can with the tools we have today.

00:06:41.595 --> 00:06:48.009
When both Dactylochlamys pisciformis and Dactylochlamys
hystriz were discovered, the ability to study

00:06:48.009 --> 00:06:51.810
them at the genetic level wasn’t available yet.

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:58.369
But this is a different era, and James was
careful with his Dactylochlamys pisciformis

00:06:58.369 --> 00:07:01.415
in part so he could study its DNA.

00:07:01.415 --> 00:07:07.619
But the stakes were still high, and so he
still needed to be careful.

00:07:07.619 --> 00:07:13.610
He took the slide with the coverslip on top
and floated it in water, letting him carefully

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:19.447
remove the coverslip without killing the precious
organism inside.

00:07:19.447 --> 00:07:25.330
Then, he needed to clean the Dactylochlamys
to make sure there was nothing else that might

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:29.409
contaminate his sample and affect his results.

00:07:29.409 --> 00:07:35.930
So he used a pipette to grab the organism
and move it over to a clean drop of water,

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:38.340
and then again to another drop.

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:45.630
And then one more, to one last drop before
storing it in a tiny tube in his freezer.

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:51.580
From there, this frozen Dactylochlamys will
have part of its DNA sequenced, revealing

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:59.004
the genes that make it the way it is and maybe
even uncovering some of its closest microbial relations.

00:07:59.004 --> 00:08:01.409
And that’s only the beginning.

00:08:01.409 --> 00:08:07.346
The first two Dactylochlamyses that James found 
took a tremendous amount of time and patience.

00:08:07.346 --> 00:08:12.183
But in the last four months, he’s found
more than twenty of them!

00:08:12.183 --> 00:08:13.770
Twenty!

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:20.369
That’s not a huge number by microbe discovery
standards, where the rapid reproduction means

00:08:20.369 --> 00:08:26.434
that you can sometimes stumble on colonies
of hundreds of a single organism.

00:08:26.434 --> 00:08:32.044
But for any dactylochlamys species, twenty
is windfall.

00:08:32.044 --> 00:08:36.960
Some of them have been frozen for their own
DNA studies, while others have been put under

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:39.450
the microscope for James to watch.

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:45.010
And sometimes he sees something new, like
this species that might be Dactylochlamys

00:08:45.010 --> 00:08:49.930
hystriz, and that seems to have extended its
tail out.

00:08:49.930 --> 00:08:56.473
Other times, the things he finds are very
new—like “new species” new.

00:08:56.473 --> 00:09:03.353
When you zoom in, you can see how similar
yet distinct it is from the other species we’ve seen.

00:09:03.353 --> 00:09:09.497
But it will still be some time before we know
enough to know whether it really is a new species.

00:09:09.497 --> 00:09:14.470
In the meantime though, the fact that we’ve
found more of these Dactylochlamyses means

00:09:14.470 --> 00:09:18.723
that we have more chances to learn more about
them.

00:09:18.723 --> 00:09:22.932
Because sure, finding a rare organism is thrilling.

00:09:22.932 --> 00:09:28.330
But even more thrilling is the chance to make
it a little less rare.

00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:34.523
The stakes of finding one may be lower, but
that doesn’t lessen the joy of discovery,

00:09:34.523 --> 00:09:40.403
of having made something feel more 
common and realizing it is still precious.

